data.content:
Yes — you should reuse the same `[MarkoutSerializable]` model and switch formatters at call time.

## Correct API

`MarkoutSerializer.Serialize` has formatter overloads:

```csharp
MarkoutSerializer.Serialize(report, Console.Out, new MarkdownFormatter(), ReleaseReportContext.Default);
MarkoutSerializer.Serialize(report, Console.Out, new PlainTextFormatter(), ReleaseReportContext.Default);
```

Equivalent instance form:

```csharp
ReleaseReportContext.Default.Serialize(report, Console.Out, new MarkdownFormatter());
ReleaseReportContext.Default.Serialize(report, Console.Out, new PlainTextFormatter());
```

## Important gotchas

### 1) `MarkoutWriterOptions` does **not** choose the formatter
Formatter is a separate argument. `MarkoutWriterOptions` only controls runtime options.

### 2) `MarkoutWriter` is **not** `IDisposable`
So don’t use `using`. Just create it and optionally call `Flush()`:

```csharp
var writer = new MarkoutWriter(Console.Out, new MarkdownFormatter());
ReleaseReportContext.Default.Serialize(report, writer);
writer.Flush();
```

### 3) `TableFormatter.Mode` does **not** exist
In `0.23.0`, pretty/tsv/jsonl mode is set on `MarkoutWriterOptions`:

```csharp
var options = new MarkoutWriterOptions
{
    TableMode = MarkoutTableMode.Pretty
};

MarkoutSerializer.Serialize(report, Console.Out, new TableFormatter(), ReleaseReportContext.Default, options);
```

## Best fit for your case

If you want:

- **Markdown with `#` heading** → `MarkdownFormatter`
- **Plain text with aligned columns** → `PlainTextFormatter` is likely the better choice

`PlainTextFormatter` supports headings + aligned field output.  
`TableFormatter` is more for compact table/table-row output, and it does **not** handle headings.

## Recommended setup

```csharp
[MarkoutSerializable(TitleProperty = nameof(Release))]
public sealed class ReleaseReport
{
    public string Release { get; init; } = "";
    public int Commits { get; init; }
    public int Contributors { get; init; }
}

[MarkoutContext(typeof(ReleaseReport))]
public partial class ReleaseReportContext : MarkoutSerializerContext { }
```

Then:

```csharp
var ctx = ReleaseReportContext.Default;

MarkoutSerializer.Serialize(report, Console.Out, new MarkdownFormatter(), ctx);
Console.WriteLine();

MarkoutSerializer.Serialize(report, Console.Out, new PlainTextFormatter(), ctx);
```

If you want, I can show the exact expected output for both formatters from this model.

